### Step 3 — Terminal: WebSocket shell bridge in server.ts
**File**: `backend/src/server.ts` — inside `attachWebSocketServer`

Add incoming message handler to the existing `connection` callback. Check `req.url` to distinguish `/ws/shell` shell connections from general status connections:

```ts
socketServer.on('connection', async (ws, req) => {
    if ((req as any).url === '/ws/shell') {
        // Shell bridge: spawn child process, pipe stdout/stderr back
        ws.on('message', (raw) => {
            try {
                const { command } = JSON.parse(raw.toString());
                if (typeof command !== 'string') return;
                const child = spawn(command, [], {
                    cwd: SystemConfig.ROOT,
                    shell: true,
                    env: process.env
                });
                child.stdout.on('data', d => ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'stdout', data: d.toString() })));
                child.stderr.on('data', d => ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'stderr', data: d.toString() })));
                child.on('close', code => ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'exit', code })));
                child.on('error', err => ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'stderr', data: err.message })));
            } catch (_) {}
        });
        return; // don't run existing status/CONCLAVE broadcast logic
    }
    // ... existing status broadcast logic unchanged
```

- `SystemConfig.ROOT` is already imported, cwd is safe
- `spawn(..., shell: true)` is required for commands like `pm2 status`, `ls`, pipes, etc.
- No hardcoded user paths — cwd is always project root

